  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none; full paper text is present.",
    "relation": "tests",
    "tranche": "T3",
    "tranche_reason": "The study compares downstream QA accuracy across models of different parameter sizes, a different measure and method from the original held-out cross-entropy scaling-law experiments.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports accuracy results across model sizes, which bear on V6. It does not measure held-out pretraining cross-entropy, fit power laws, test extrapolation, or compare compute-optimal allocations. Kaplan et al. is cited as prior work, not tested here. Other reported metrics, such as correction and overthinking rates and token consumption, do not directly measure the claim versions.",
    "test_reason": "The study evaluates off-the-shelf Qwen 2.5, LLaMA 3 and Gemma 2 size series on MMLU, and reports accuracy rising with parameter count. This bears weakly on V6 in a new setting: cross-model comparison with prompted fast and slow modes. It fits no predictive relation and does not use pretraining loss. It is not an original source. It does not test V1–V5 or V7.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly right. The abstract and Section 3.4 say parameter scaling improves both knowledge and reasoning, with knowledge gains larger and more sustained; reasoning gains are mainly from 1B to 8B. The Conclusion section contradicts this, saying 'with reasoning adjustment more significant', which is likely a typo. The authors also say scaling makes reasoning much more prudent (less overthinking) and only modestly better at correction. They make no claim about predictive scaling laws.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study compares QA accuracy across existing model sizes and prompted cognitive modes, rather than training language models across controlled parameter, token, or compute budgets and fitting held-out loss curves.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Reported outcomes distinguish fast and slow prompting modes; the slow-mode setup differs for reasoning models and other models, as described in Appendix A.3.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Reasoning gain (slow minus fast accuracy) is non-monotonic in size. It peaks at medium size, e.g. Qwen 4.0 at 7B vs 1.5 at 32B, and LLaMA 5.4 at 8B vs 1.3 at 70B. Overthinking rate falls steeply with size (LLaMA 52.6→7.2).",
        "proposed_claim": "The benefit of chain-of-thought prompting over direct answering does not grow monotonically with parameter count; it saturates or shrinks beyond about 8B.",
        "row_ids": []
      }
    ],
    "controls": "n",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "n",
    "code_released": "The paper says datasets and code will be publicly released; no URL is stated.",
    "peer_reviewed": "AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (the paper text identifies the version as a preprint under review).",
    "problems": "The study does not use a controlled training sweep, held-out pretraining loss, compute or token measurements, or scaling-law fits. Size comparisons use different existing models and families; results are limited to multiple-choice QA and the reported models. No uncertainty estimates are given.",
    "rows_left_out": "No additional rows omitted from the kept Table 1 MMLU accuracy results. Other tables and figures report reasoning gains, correction/overthinking rates, token consumption, domain-specific scores, or anchoring results rather than direct tests of the claim versions cataloged here.",
